  faqs: [
    { q: 'Is homeschooling legal in North Macedonia?', a: 'Parental-choice homeschooling is not established in North Macedonian law. Primary education is compulsory and the parent or guardian is obliged to enable the child to fulfil that obligation. The law\'s home provision covers students who cannot attend for more than three weeks because of greater health problems or chronic illness, with teaching organised in home conditions or a health institution and the parent informing the school.' },
    { q: 'Is there a post-15 window like in Croatia, Albania, Greece, Serbia, or Bulgaria?', a: 'No — and this is the most important difference between North Macedonia and its neighbours. Compulsory education here extends through the secondary level. Any provider recycling a post-15 pitch from a neighbouring country is describing another country\'s law. Because the scope of compulsory education can be amended, confirm the current position with the Ministry of Education and Science.' },
    { q: 'So what can Smartious actually offer us?', a: 'One configuration, run properly: supplementary study alongside your school enrolment, throughout both primary and secondary. The school carries every legal obligation; we teach Cambridge or IB subjects live in the after-school slot toward external examinations.' },
    { q: 'We are returning from Germany or Switzerland mid-curriculum — what are our options?', a: 'Enrol locally, which carries the legal obligation, and keep the international pathway running alongside it with us — the same curriculum, teachers, and examination board continuing across the move, rather than restarting inside a new system.' },
    { q: 'We are not resident in North Macedonia — does the obligation apply?', a: 'It attaches to children resident here. Non-resident families follow their country of residence\'s framework, which in this region can differ sharply a short drive away. Where the line sits for your household is a question for your own advisers.' },
    { q: 'How does Smartious compare with NOVA, QSI, or the Yahya Kemal colleges?', a: 'They are established campuses with in-person culture and a local peer group we cannot replicate. Smartious runs live small-group teaching at USD 2,160-6,480 a year — the supplement beside any school, and the only complete option outside Skopje.' },
    { q: 'Where do North Macedonian students sit Cambridge examinations?', a: 'At authorised provision confirmed per family per session — Skopje first, with Greek, Bulgarian, Serbian, and Albanian regional options worth checking for border families, since no part of the country is far from a frontier.' },
    { q: 'Can my child access North Macedonian universities with A-Levels or an IB Diploma?', a: 'Universities admit foreign secondary qualifications through recognition procedures with requirements confirmed per programme, and the English-medium tier — South East European University at Tetovo and University American College Skopje — reads Cambridge and IB qualifications more directly. UCAS and the Common Application stand open internationally.' },
    { q: 'How does live class scheduling work given the time difference?', a: 'North Macedonia runs CET (UTC+1) in winter and CEST (UTC+2) in summer — only 1-2 hours behind Nairobi EAT — so live teaching lands naturally inside the school day, with the after-school slot suiting supplementary students.' },
    { q: 'Which parts of North Macedonia does Smartious cover?', a: 'Skopje, Tetovo and the Polog valley, Bitola and Pelagonia, Ohrid and the lake, Kumanovo and the north-east, and Štip and the east have dedicated pages with local context. Live online delivery works identically anywhere in the country — which outside Skopje is precisely the point.' },
  ],

  ctaH: 'Book an assessment for your child',
  ctaSubhead: 'Two-gate admissions process. Submit the assessment request form — our Head of Admissions reviews every request within three business days. If accepted, the assessment fee is invoiced before the diagnostic is scheduled. One thing worth knowing before you write: in North Macedonia we work alongside your school rather than instead of it, at every age — the law here is stricter than in the countries next door, and we would rather say so now than later.',
